"Internal Error ()\nciteturn21view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.joelchan.me/assets/pdf/2018-cscw-schema-highlighter.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://www.joelchan.me/assets/pdf/2018-cscw-schema-highlighter.pdf: (400) Timeout fetching\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAccelerating Innovation Through Analogy Mining (https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2018/0736.pdf)\nciteturn21view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 5; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2018/0736.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 426\nL0@P0: Accelerating Innovation Through Analogy Mining\nL1@P0: Tom Hope1, Joel Chan2, Aniket Kittur2and Dafna Shahaf1\nL2@P0: 1 The Hebrew University of Jerusalem\nL3@P0: 2 Carnegie Mellon University\nL4@P0: tom.hope@mail.huji.ac.il, joelchan@umd.edu, nkittur@cs.cmu.edu, dshahaf@cs.huji.ac.il\nL5@P0: Abstract\nL6@P0: The availability of large idea repositories (e.g.,\nL7@P0: patents) could significantly accelerate innovation\nL8@P0: and discovery by providing people inspiration from\nL9@P0: solutions to analogous problems. However, find\u0002ing useful analogies in these large, messy, real\u0002world repositories remains a persistent challenge\nL10@P0: for both humans and computers. Previous ap\u0002proaches include costly hand-created databases that\nL11@P0: do not scale, or machine-learning similarity met\u0002rics that struggle to account for structural similar\u0002ity, which is central to analogy. In this paper1\nL12@P0: we explore the viability and value of learning sim\u0002ple structural representations. Our approach com\u0002bines crowdsourcing and recurrent neural networks\nL13@P0: to extract purpose and mechanism vector represen\u0002tations from product descriptions. We demonstrate\nL14@P0: that these learned vectors allow us to find analo\u0002gies with higher precision and recall than tradi\u0002tional methods. In an ideation experiment, analo\u0002gies retrieved by our models significantly increased\nL15@P0: people’s likelihood of generating creative ideas.\nL16@P0: 1 Introduction\nL17@P0: The ability to find useful analogies is critical to driving in\u0002novation in a variety of domains. Many important discov\u0002eries in science were driven by analogies: for example, an\nL18@P0: analogy between bacteria and slot machines helped Salvador\nL19@P0: Luria advance the theory of bacterial mutation. An analogy\nL20@P0: to a bicycle allowed the Wright brothers to design a steer\u0002able aircraft. Whether architecture, design, technology, art, or\nL21@P0: mathematics, the ability to find and apply patterns from other\nL22@P0: domains is fundamental to human achievement [Hesse, 1966;\nL23@P0: Markman and Loewenstein, 2010; Dahl and Moreau, 2002].\nL24@P0: The explosion of available online data represents an un\u0002precedented opportunity to find new analogies and accelerate\nL25@P0: human progress across domains. The US Patent database has\nL26@P0: full text for more than 9 million patents. Millions of scientific\nL27@P0: papers and legal cases are searchable on the web. Websites\nL28@P0: like InnoCentive2, Quirky3and OpenIDEO4contain millions\nL29@P0: of ideas, problems and solutions.\nL30@P0: 1\nL31@P0: See full version [Hope et al., 2017]\nL32@P0: 2\nL33@P0: innocentive.com\nL34@P0: 3\nL35@P0: quirky.com 4 OpenIDEO.com\nL36@P0: We believe these datasets form a treasure trove of analogies\nL37@P0: that can accelerate problem solving, innovation and discov\u0002ery. In a striking recent example, a car mechanic invented a\nL38@P0: simple device to ease difficult childbirths by drawing an anal\u0002ogy to extracting a cork from a wine bottle, which he discov\u0002ered in a YouTube video. We imagine a future in which peo\u0002ple could search through data based on deep analogical simi\u0002larity rather than simple keywords; lawyers or legal scholars\nL39@P0: could find legal precedents sharing similar systems of rela\u0002tions to a contemporary case; and product or service design\u0002ers could mine myriad potential solutions to their problem.\nL40@P0: However, sifting through massive data sources to find\nL41@P0: analogies poses a serious challenge for both humans and ma\u0002chines. In humans, memory retrieval is highly sensitive to\nL42@P0: surface similarity, favoring near, within-domain analogs that\nL43@P0: share object attributes over far, structurally similar analogs\nL44@P0: that share object relations [Gentner et al., 1985; Holyoak\nL45@P0: and Thagard, 1996; Gentner et al., 1993; Gick and Holyoak,\nL46@P0: 1983]. Analogical processing also incurs a heavy cognitive\nL47@P0: load, taxing working memory when even a few relations are\nL48@P0: required to be processed [Halford et al., 2005].\nL49@P0: Finding analogies is challenging for machines as well, as\nL50@P0: it is based on having an understanding of the deep relational\nL51@P0: similarity between two entities that may be very different in\nL52@P0: terms of surface attributes [Gentner, 1983]. Recent advances\nL53@P0: in data mining and information retrieval use vector represen\u0002tations in order to calculate similarity measures [Mikolov et\nL54@P0: al., 2013; Deerwester et al., 1990; Blei et al., 2003]. These\nL55@P0: approaches excel at detecting surface similarity, but are often\nL56@P0: unable to detect similarity between documents whose word\nL57@P0: distributions are disparate. The problem is especially acute\nL58@P0: when the source and target domains are different (e.g., bacte\u0002rial mutation and slot machines).\nL59@P0: In this paper, we are interested in automatically discover\u0002ing analogies in large, unstructured data sets. In particular,\nL60@P0: we focus on a corpus of product innovations. There are two\nL61@P0: insights behind our approach that we believe may make this\nL62@P0: problem tractable despite its longstanding status as a “holy\nL63@P0: grail” in both cognitive science and AI. First, rather than try\u0002ing to solve the problem of fully structured analogical reason\u0002ing, we instead explore the idea that we can use weaker struc\u0002tural representations that can be learned and reasoned with at\nL64@P0: scale (in other words, there is a tradeoff between the ease of\nL65@P0: extraction of a structure and its expressivity). Specifically, we\nL66@P0: investigate the weaker structural representation of an idea’s\nL67@P0: Proceedings of the Twenty-Seventh International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI-18)\nL68@P0-1: 5274purpose and mechanism as a way to find useful analogies.\nL69@P1: The second insight is that advances in crowdsourcing have\nL70@P1: made it possible to harvest rich signals of analogical struc\u0002ture that can help machine learning models learn in ways that\nL71@P1: would not be possible with existing datasets alone.\nL72@P1: Our approach uses the behavioral traces of crowd work\u0002ers searching for analogies and identifying the purpose and\nL73@P1: mechanisms of ideas, then developing machine learning mod\u0002els and similarity metrics suited for analogy mining. We\nL74@P1: demonstrate that our methods allow us to find analogies\nL75@P1: with higher precision and recall than traditional information\u0002retrieval methods. In a user study, we show that we can gener\u0002ate far analogies that “inspire” participants to generate more\nL76@P1: innovative ideas than alternative baselines, increasing the rel\u0002ative proportion of positively-rated ideas by at least 25%.\nL77@P1: 2 Learning a Representation for Analogies\nL78@P1: 2.1 Motivation\nL79@P1: Much work in computation analogy has focused on fully\nL80@P1: structured data, often with logic-based representations. For\nL81@P1: example [Falkenhainer et al., 1989],\nL82@P1: CAUSE(GREATER-THAN[TEMPERATURE(coffee),\nL83@P1: TEMPERATURE (ice-cube)],\nL84@P1: FLOW(coffee, ice-cube, heat, bar))\nL85@P1: These representations, while very expressive, are notori\u0002ously difficult to obtain. In this section, our goal is to come\nL86@P1: up with a weaker representation that can be learned, while\nL87@P1: still being expressive enough to allow analogical mining.\nL88@P1: Analogies between product ideas are intricately related to\nL89@P1: their purpose and mechanism. Informally, a product’s pur\u0002pose is “what it does, what it is used for”, and a product’s\nL90@P1: mechanism is “how it does it, how it works”. The importance\nL91@P1: of purpose and mechanism is theoretically rooted in early\nL92@P1: cognitive psychology work on schema induction, which de\u0002fine the core components of a schema as a goal and proposed\nL93@P1: solution to it [Gick and Holyoak, 1983]. More recently, the\nL94@P1: practical value of defining a problem schema as a purpose and\nL95@P1: mechanism has been demonstrated to have empirical benefits\nL96@P1: in finding and using analogies to augment idea generation [Yu\nL97@P1: et al., 2016b; 2016a; 2014b; 2014a].\nL98@P1: Separating an idea into purpose and mechanisms enables\nL99@P1: core analogical innovation processes. Assume (for the mo\u0002ment) that we have for each product i two vectors, pi and mi\nL100@P1: ,\nL101@P1: representing the product’s purpose and mechanism, respec\u0002tively. Using this representation, we can apply rich queries to\nL102@P1: our corpus of products, such as:\nL103@P1: Same purpose, different mechanism. Given the cor\u0002pus of all products P, a product i with (normalized) pur\u0002pose and mechanism vectors pi\nL104@P1: , mi, and distance metrics\nL105@P1: dp(·, ·), dm(·, ·) between purpose and mechanism vectors (re\u0002spectively), solve:\nL106@P1: argmin ˜idp(pi, p˜i) s.t. dm(mi, m˜i) ≥ threshold, (1)\nL107@P1: Same Mechanism, different purpose (re-purposing). Solve:\nL108@P1: argmin ˜idm(mi, m˜i) s.t. dp(pi, p˜i) ≥ threshold (2)\nL109@P1: Importantly, our dataset of product descriptions contains\nL110@P1: noisy texts, often written informally by non-professional peo\u0002ple. In these texts product descriptions are often lacking de\u0002tail or are ill-defined. To automatically describe a product in\nL111@P1: Figure 1: Collecting purpose, mechanism annotations.\nL112@P1: terms of a richer, formal functional model such as in engineer\u0002ing research [Hirtz et al., 2002; Ookubo et al., 2007] would\nL113@P1: require an inordinate amount of meticulous data annotation\nL114@P1: and collection by professional engineers.\nL115@P1: 2.2 Data\nL116@P1: Innovation Corpus. We test our approach with a corpus\nL117@P1: of product descriptions from Quirky.com, an online crowd\u0002sourced product innovation website. Quirky is representa\u0002tive of the kinds of datasets we are interested in because it is\nL118@P1: large (at the time of writing, it hosts upwards of 10,000 prod\u0002uct ideas, of which our corpus included 8500), unstructured\nL119@P1: (ideas are described in natural language), and covers a variety\nL120@P1: of domains which makes cross-domain analogies possible.\nL121@P1: Collecting Purpose and Mechanism Data. We needed to\nL122@P1: collect analogy-specific data to train our model. We aim\nL123@P1: to develop a lightweight task that avoids complex structured\nL124@P1: representations, so we can scale up the collection of annota\u0002tions through the use of crowdsourcing [Kittur et al., 2008].\nL125@P1: We show Amazon Mechanical Turk (AMT) crowd workers a\nL126@P1: product description, asking them to annotate the parts of the\nL127@P1: text related to the purposes and mechanisms of the product.\nL128@P1: We frame the problem in simple terms, guiding workers to\nL129@P1: look for chunks of text talking about “what the product does,\nL130@P1: what it is good for” (purposes), and “how it works, what are\nL131@P1: its components” (mechanisms). See Figure 1.\nL132@P1: 2.3 Method\nL133@P1: Extracting Purpose and Mechanism vectors\nL134@P1: In this section, we describe our approach to learning to\nL135@P1: extract purpose and mechanism product representations.\nL136@P1: We begin with a set of N training product texts XN =\nL137@P1: {x1, x2, . . . , xN }, where each xiis a variable-length se\u0002quence of tokens (x\nL138@P1: 1\nL139@P1: i\nL140@P1: , x2\nL141@P1: i\nL142@P1: , . . . , xT\nL143@P1: i\nL144@P1: ). For each document xi,\nL145@P1: we collect a set of K purpose annotations and K mechanism\nL146@P1: annotations, where K is the number of workers who anno\u0002tate each document. We define the purpose annotation to be\nL147@P1: a binary vector ˜pik = (˜p\nL148@P1: 1\nL149@P1: ik\nL150@P1: , p˜\nL151@P1: 2\nL152@P1: ik\nL153@P1: , . . . , p˜\nL154@P1: T\nL155@P1: ik\nL156@P1: ) of the same length\nL157@P1: as xi, with p˜\nL158@P1: j\nL159@P1: ik = 1 if token x\nL160@P1: j\nL161@P1: i\nL162@P1: is annotated as purpose by\nL163@P1: annotator k, p˜\nL164@P1: j\nL165@P1: ik = 0 if not. In the same way, we denote the\nL166@P1: mechanism annotation with ˜mik = ( ˜m1\nL167@P1: ik\nL168@P1: , m˜\nL169@P1: 2\nL170@P1: ik\nL171@P1: , . . . , m˜\nL172@P1: T\nL173@P1: ik\nL174@P1: ).\nL175@P1: We apply a simple and soft aggregation of the K annota\u0002tions. In simple terms, we look at all words that were an\u0002notated, and take a TF-IDF-weighted average of their GloVe\nL176@P1: [Pennington et al., 2014] word vectors (pre-trained on Com\u0002mon Crawl web data), resulting in weighted-average vectors\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://groups.psych.northwestern.edu/gentner/papers/Gentner83.2b.pdf)\nciteturn21view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 9; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://groups.psych.northwestern.edu/gentner/papers/Gentner83.2b.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 514\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBERTMap: A BERT-Based Ontology Alignmen